{"step_index":155,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T11:32:48Z","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Specificity**\n\nI'm focusing intently on tool specificity. I've been refining my approach to avoid unnecessary use of bash commands for file creation or appending, opting for dedicated tools like `>` and `>>` respectively. I am also working to use `grep_search` more for searching, rather than general grep commands. I must remember to use the more efficient tools.\n\n\n**Considering Data Persistence Options**\n\nI'm now investigating data persistence within the Streamlit application. The immediate hurdle is the transient nature of cloud-based deployments. Local files are out unless there's a database connection. Considering the use case, session state and query parameters look like viable options, although I need to consider their limitations. Streamlit's experimental features also present some promise. I must remember to assess these approaches carefully.\n\n\n**Evaluating Storage Approaches**\n\nI'm now contemplating several storage strategies for the portfolio data. Initially, I'm leaning toward a simple JSON file, `portfolio.json`, offering easy local persistence. However, for Streamlit Cloud, I'm weighing options. A simple cloud storage service could work, or instructing the user to commit changes to the Python file via Git. The 'Portfolio Manager' UI idea has me leaning towards git commits to keep online version up-to-date.\n\n\n**Implementing Data Handling**\n\nI'm now in the thick of implementing data handling for the portfolio manager. The core focus is on reading and writing to a `portfolio.json` file. For local runs, this provides persistence. I've integrated a simple JSON file read/write functionality, along with an initial file load and saving function. My next step is replacing the hardcoded data in the UI with the data loaded from this file, ensuring that the \"My Portfolio\" tab accurately reflects the saved state. I will then add a UI form to add new stocks.\n\n\n**Refining Portfolio Management**\n\nI've just added the ability to clear and manage stocks within the portfolio. This involves loading and saving the portfolio as a JSON object. I've integrated a simple UI with a radio selection to switch markets. I'm now focusing on ensuring data persistence is seamless for each update made within the UI.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"replace_file_content","args":{"AllowMultiple":false,"Description":"Add load and save portfolio functionality using a JSON file.","EndLine":146,"Instruction":"Add json import and portfolio management logic. Update tab2 to use the saved portfolio.","ReplacementContent":"import json\nimport os\n\ndef load_portfolio():\n    if os.path.exists(\"portfolio.json\"):\n        try:\n            with open(\"portfolio.json\", \"r\") as f:\n                return json.load(f)\n        except:\n            pass\n    return {\"US Stocks\": \"AAPL, TSLA@200, PLTR\", \"Malaysia Stocks\": \"SUNCON@3.00, MAYBANK, TENAGA\"}\n\ndef save_portfolio(data):\n    with open(\"portfolio.json\", \"w\") as f:\n        json.dump(data, f)\n\nportfolio_data = load_portfolio()\n\n# Tabs for different modes\ntab1, tab2 = st.tabs([\"🎯 Discovery Scanner (Find New Buys)\", \"💼 My Portfolio (Check Holds/Sells)\"])\n\nwith tab1:\n    st.markdown(\"### Market Discovery Scanner\")","StartLine":134,"TargetContent":"# Tabs for different modes\ntab1, tab2 = st.tabs([\"🎯 Discovery Scanner (Find New Buys)\", \"💼 My Portfolio (Check Holds/Sells)\"])\n\nwith tab1:\n    st.markdown(\"### Market Discovery Scanner\")","TargetFile":"C:/Users/wai_j/.gemini/antigravity/brain/c155f291-512d-4565-8630-f27b8bc949d1/stock_screener.py","TargetLintErrorIds":[],"toolAction":"Adding portfolio logic","toolSummary":"Add portfolio save feature"}}]}
